<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Friday!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize short stories using drawings or pictures. Communication: Explain simple written instructions to peers, using English or Spanish.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize short stories using drawings or pictures (e.g., draws a sun and says "Sunday" to show the story's setting). Can explain simple written instructions to peers, using gestures (e.g., points to the calendar to show, "Today is Monday."). Can clarify simple instructions, using basic gestures (e.g., gives thumbs up to indicate "yes").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, class! Today is Friday!" and points to a large calendar, circling "Friday". The teacher asks, "What day is today?" and students chorally respond "Friday!". The teacher then asks, "What was yesterday?" (Thursday) and "What will be tomorrow?" (Saturday) using gestures and pointing to the calendar. The teacher shows flashcards of weather (sunny, rainy, cloudy) and asks, "What's the weather like today?" and "What was the weather like yesterday?" Students respond with one-word answers or simple phrases (e.g., "Sunny!", "It was rainy."). This activates prior knowledge of days and weather, connecting to the theme "Today Is Friday!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to summarize a simple daily event with a drawing and a simple phrase. The teacher says, "Listen! Yesterday, it was sunny. I played outside." The teacher quickly draws a sun and a stick figure playing on the board. The teacher then writes "Yesterday: Sunny. Play." The teacher then models explaining simple instructions using gestures and clear, simple English, for example: "Look, I draw. (Teacher draws a cloud) It is cloudy. You draw a cloud." The teacher also models how to clarify simple instructions with gestures, e.g., pointing to the ear for "listen" or thumbs up for "yes, understand."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'summarize' and 'explain' through simple questions and gestures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we use pictures to tell a story?" (Teacher gestures drawing) "Yes or no?" (Students thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'draw a sun,' can you show me with your hands?" (Teacher makes drawing motion, students mimic). "Do you understand?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative excerpt (teacher-read short story), picture book, or large flashcards depicting school activities and weather for different days of the week. Example story: "My School Week".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher shows a sequence of large, simple drawings or a short picture story about a child's week at school. For example:
                    <br>Picture 1: Monday, a boy learning Math. Caption: "On Monday, I learn math. It was sunny."
                    <br>Picture 2: Tuesday, a girl reading a book. Caption: "On Tuesday, I read a book. It was cloudy."
                    <br>Picture 3: Wednesday, children doing science. Caption: "On Wednesday, we have science. It was rainy."
                    <br>Picture 4: Thursday, children playing with friends. Caption: "Yesterday was Thursday. I played with my friends. It was sunny."
                    <br>Picture 5: Friday, children drawing. Caption: "Today is Friday! I like art! It is sunny."
                The teacher reads each short sentence clearly, pointing to the pictures and emphasizing the day, activity, and weather vocabulary. Pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" is highlighted through repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about schedules: Communicating about daily classroom activities and schedules using appropriate language and turn-taking (e.g., "Today is Monday."). Answering questions about days: "Yesterday was Wednesday." Expressing preferences: "I like Friday. Art is on Friday!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> After reading, the teacher asks, "What days are in the story?" (Students point to days on a calendar). "What did the boy do on Monday?" (Students point to math picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (TPR):</b> The teacher says a sentence, "On Wednesday, it was sunny." If true, students stand up. If false, students sit down. (Based on the story read).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher holds up a picture (e.g., rainy weather) and asks, "What day was it rainy?" Students try to say the day or point to the day on the calendar.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Past simple with "be" ("Yesterday was...", "It was..."), Present simple with "be" ("Today is...", "It is..."), WH-questions ("What is it?"). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, calendar, schedule, activity, week, school, friend, math, science, English, sunny, rainy, cloudy, yesterday, today. Pragmatic: Answering questions about days, expressing preferences. Pronunciation: /w/, /f/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice – Match and Summarize:</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet. On one side, there are simple pictures (e.g., a sun, a cloud, a book, a math symbol). On the other side, there are short, incomplete sentences (e.g., "It was ____ yesterday.", "On ____, I read."). Students draw lines to match the pictures to the correct sentences. Then, they complete the sentences using given word banks (sunny, rainy, cloudy, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, learn, read, draw). For example, they match a sun picture to "It was ____ yesterday" and write "sunny."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled – Picture Explanation:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards depicting a day, an activity, and weather (e.g., Wednesday, Science, Rainy). The teacher prompts, "What day was it? What did we do? What was the weather?" Students answer using simple sentences like "It was Wednesday. We had science. It was rainy." The teacher then models, "Show me 'Wednesday'." Students point to Wednesday on the calendar. "Show me 'rainy'." Students make a falling rain gesture. The teacher instructs students, "Now, show your friend what was on Wednesday with gestures. Tell your friend."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction – "My Story Summary":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a small picture strip showing one day of the week, one activity, and one weather condition (e.g., "Tuesday - English - Cloudy"). Without showing the picture, Student A draws a simple picture to summarize their strip (e.g., a cloud, a book for English, Tuesday written above). Then, Student A shows their drawing to Student B and tries to explain: "It was Tuesday. It was cloudy. I learn English." Student B listens and guesses, "Was it cloudy?" Student A clarifies with a thumbs up or shake of the head, and may add a gesture. They then swap roles. The teacher circulates, facilitating and providing vocabulary support.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative project work and peer explanation. Students will continue working on their "Weekly Story Journal" project. This is the integration phase where they apply their summarization and explanation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Journal Entry Creation (Individual):</b> Each student takes out their "Weekly Story Journal" (a simple notebook or pre-printed pages). The teacher reviews the model journal entry: "Today is Friday! It is sunny." (with a sun drawing). The teacher instructs students to choose one day from this week (e.g., Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday) and think about the weather and one activity from that day. They will create a new journal entry for that day, using a simple drawing to summarize the weather and activity, and writing 1-3 words/phrases as a caption (e.g., "Monday: Rainy, Math class.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Peer Explanation & Clarification (Pairs):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A shows their new journal entry (drawing + caption) to Student B. Student A explains their entry using simple English (e.g., "Yesterday was Thursday. It was sunny. I played with my friends.") and gestures (e.g., pointing to the sun drawing, mimicking playing). Student B listens and can ask simple WH-questions like "What day?" or "What weather?" or "What activity?". Student A practices clarifying by repeating, using different gestures, or pointing. Then, students swap roles. The teacher circulates, offering support, prompting questions, and encouraging the use of target vocabulary and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Explaining an Instruction (Teacher-led):</b> The teacher writes a simple instruction on the board, e.g., "Draw a rainy cloud." The teacher asks one student to come forward and explain this instruction to the class using only gestures and a few simple English words if necessary (e.g., "You... draw... rain..."). The class follows the instructions based on the explanation.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Weekly Story Journal" presentation and peer explanation, and during the instruction explanation task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Journal Presentation:</b> Each student presents one of their "Weekly Story Journal" entries (drawing + caption) to the teacher or a small group of peers. They verbally summarize the entry (e.g., "Yesterday was Wednesday. It was rainy. I learn math.") and use their drawing as a visual aid.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Instruction Explanation:</b> The teacher gives each student a simple instruction written on a card (e.g., "Write 'Friday'," "Draw a happy face," "Say 'Today is..."). The student must explain this instruction to a peer using gestures and simple English, without showing the card. The peer attempts to follow the instruction.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarization:</b> Can summarize short stories using drawings or pictures (e.g., draws a sun and says "Sunday" to show the story's setting).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Explanation:</b> Can explain simple written instructions to peers, using gestures (e.g., points to the calendar to show, "Today is Monday.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Can clarify simple instructions, using basic gestures (e.g., gives thumbs up to indicate "yes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Uses target vocabulary (days of the week, weather adjectives, school activities, time markers like "yesterday," "today").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Attempts to use "was" for past events and "is" for present events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear enough pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday."</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I draw a picture and tell my friend about a day and the weather? (Thumbs up / Thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I show my friend how to do something with my hands? (Yes / No).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students to think about the lesson and respond to the following questions:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for me today? (e.g., drawing, saying "Friday")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was a little difficult for me today? (e.g., explaining with gestures, saying "Wednesday")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., students effectively used drawings to summarize, peer explanations with gestures were clear).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to better summarize stories or explain instructions in the future? (e.g., more sentence frames for verbal summaries, more practice with specific gestures for common instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students engaged with the "Weekly Story Journal" project as a mediation tool? How can I further integrate drawing and simple written summarization?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson completes the "School Days" theme and the "Weekly Story Journal" project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, likely starting with Lesson 1 (Listening & Vocab Intro) of the new sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>